---
import config from '../config.json';
import EventRow from './EventRow.astro';
import PhotoSlot from './PhotoSlot.astro';
import {
  content, upcoming, value, filled, copiedOn, formatDate, provenanceStamp,
  scheduleConfigured,
} from '../lib/content.ts';
// The window rules live outside this component so the gate that reads the built page (`scripts/check-schedule-window.mjs`)
// can compare it against the same rule rather than a copy of it (D238).
import { todayIn, snapshot } from '../lib/events.mjs';
import { HORIZON_WEEKS, MAX_ROWS, scheduleWindow } from '../lib/event-window.mjs';
import type { UpcomingItem } from '../lib/content.ts';
// Round-7 review (2026-11-18), `/dates`/Remove: the reach sentence leaves the page (D431), so the page no longer
// computes a file window. `feedWindow`, `publishableRows` and `windowSentence` keep exactly one consumer each -
// `scripts/check-schedule-window.mjs` (which still checks that a reach sentence the page *does* print is the
// generated one) and `src/lib/ics.mjs` (which puts it in every published `DESCRIPTION`). An import that has no
// call behind it is how a future session would re-add a sentence that no row owns.
// The rhythm is generated from the rows this build publishes (D383), by the same module the home hero calls,
// so `/dates`'s opening line and the hero's clause cannot be edited apart.
import { rhythmSentence } from '../lib/rhythm.mjs';
import SignUpLinks from './SignUpLinks.astro';
import { PLATFORMS } from '../lib/rsvp.mjs';
import { newTabQualifier } from '../lib/links.mjs';

/**
 * When we meet — the schedule, in two widths of one component.
 *
 * What it replaced, and why the replacement had to be written rather than enabled:
 * `.notes/roadmap-events.md` measured `UpcomingMeetups.astro` as "**entirely invented placeholder
 * content**" — "Saturday, 2:00 PM", "Local Library - Study Room B", "Topic: Introduction to Group
 * Theory", a "Reserve Your Spot" button — and said any schedule work should **replace** it, not turn it
 * on. It was also pre-design CSS (`bg-blue-50`, `text-gray-900`, `shadow-lg`) and it pointed at
 * `/math-study-session.jpg`, a file that is not in `public/`, so switching the flag on would have both
 * published fiction about a real club and failed the build. `PastEvents.astro` was `<h2>Past Events</h2>`
 * and a comment. Both flags were `false` in `src/config.json`, and prod agrees — the curl numbers are in
 * the roadmap note.
 *
 * The three states, all designed, none of them an invented event (record 0010: missing content is a state,
 * never a blank, never a build failure; DESIGN.md "States"):
 *   - **dates exist** → rows: date lane, series name, status word. D92: the site publishes **series names
 *     and real dates**, never a frequency.
 *   - **a source is configured and holds nothing** → the round-7 empty state the operator chose (option A as
 *     measured then: 305px, status word + title + two-line body + a 48px primary and a 40px secondary — D139
 *     has since retired the 40px step for buttons — + the dated line, "two paths out"), because a guaranteed
 *     condition of an upcoming-only feed is a gap between events.
 *   - **no source configured yet** (D132 rules the source is Mobilizon or an ICS feed, and the committed
 *     snapshot says it was read from one) → the same block, with its own status line, and the bracketed
 *     `[venue blurb]` from `src/content.json` **not rendered**: a bracket is a hole in our knowledge, and
 *     DESIGN.md keeps brackets off the page until a source exists. `scripts/check-content.mjs` fails a
 *     build that ships one.
 *
 * Controls, after the D139 ruling (2026-11-03):
 *   - One control per band: the **48px primary** in the hero's own grammar (`px-8 py-4`, no hard-coded
 *     height — DESIGN.md: "heights are derived, not chosen"), declaring `data-control="primary"` because the
 *     height rules now match the name the source publishes (D140).
 *   - Every other path is an **inline link, not a second button.** It was written while the gate still demanded
 *     a 40px secondary and then forbade one (record 0016); the ruling retired 40px for buttons, so a second
 *     button would now be legal at 48px. It stays a link anyway: DESIGN.md prices an in-sentence path at its
 *     24px line, and a notice-shaped block reads better with one pressable block and one path in prose than
 *     with two buttons of the same size.
 * Every path goes somewhere that exists — D117's rule is that a path needs a page (that is what killed
 * `Schedule` and `Members` from the nav). As of 2026-11-07 the `Schedule` path has a page:
 * `src/pages/schedule.astro`, so the nav label is back with something behind it.
 *
 * 2026-11-07, the split (D227). The homepage used to print up to four rows, which made the landing page a
 * calendar and left the calendar with no home: `/events.ics` existed, `/schedule` did not, and a visitor who
 * wanted "what else is coming?" had one answer only — paste a URL into a calendar client. Now:
 *   - `variant="full"` (the `/schedule` page) lists every open date in the snapshot — **within a window**
 *     (D238). The "no cap" this line used to assert was true for one day: an uncapped list is a page that grows
 *     with every date a host creates, and D224 priced one art-bearing row at **+197px of page**. What this
 *     variant lists is `HORIZON_WEEKS` weeks ahead, at most `MAX_ROWS` rows, both from
 *     `src/lib/event-window.mjs`, and the page says out loud what it left out. `/events.ics` stays uncapped:
 *     the limit is a page-layout rule, not a fact about the club's calendar.
 *   - `variant="compact"` (the homepage, `src/pages/index.astro`) prints **no date at all** and points at the page
 *     of record. Since 2026-11-14 that is the operator's rule, not a layout choice: the `/join` objective says
 *     "`/schedule` stays the only surface that prints dates", and `check:dated-surfaces` enforces it on every
 *     built page (D306 supersedes the date half of D227; the join mechanics D228 absorbed here stay).
 *   - `limit` is the prop that says how many rows, so the two call sites read the same `upcoming()` and
 *     cannot drift into two calendars. `check:ics` and `check:content` are what keep the page and
 *     `/events.ics` honest about the dates themselves.
 *
 * 2026-11-07, the second half of the same change (D228): the compact width **absorbed the join band**
 * (`JoinCommunity.astro`, "Come once / How it works"), which is deleted. Why merged rather than left beside
 * it: the two bands answered one question in two places — one said "here is a date", the other said "here is
 * how you come to a date" — with the horizon band between them, so a reader met the promise and its mechanics
 * three screens apart. The landscape is now hero → what we take up → the horizon → **come once, and here is the
 * page where the dates are** → footer: D306 took the date out of this band on 2026-11-14, so what the merge
 * bought (the mechanics, in the place a reader is deciding to come) is what remains.
 *
 * The compact width no longer draws a card, a leaf or a cluster (D306): `/schedule` is the only surface that
 * prints a date, and the homepage points at it. The full width's rows stay ruled strips (D220's `edges`),
 * because there the dates, not one date, are the thing.
 *
 * The rhythm inside the full width is R6's `rows` option, ruled 2026-11-05 (D214; the pair's measurement is
 * D220): 24px below the heading (was 48), 16px between rows (was 24), 8px of row padding (was 12). Round 15
 * priced the option at **-88px of page at 1440/1240/1024 and -120px at 640/390/320, with the emptiness
 * falling 36-73px** @measured 2026-11-04 by `npm run check:rhythm`; as shipped with `edges`, this section
 * alone measured **80px shorter at 1240** (852 -> 772) @measured 2026-11-05 by `npm run check:space` - that is
 * the **one-row** section, not today's: with two art-bearing rows it is **968.9px tall at 1240** with a
 * **144px** biggest run inside it @measured 2026-11-06 by `npm run check:space`. A row costs roughly what a
 * plate costs: +197px of page for the second card (D224). With these values in, the sweep re-prices `rows` at
 * 0px/0px at all six widths - the row stays as a drift guard.
 *
 * A `STATUS:CANCELLED` row is **published and not shown**: `isUpcoming` drops it from this list, while
 * `events.ics` keeps it with its UID and a raised SEQUENCE so a subscriber's client retires its copy (D223,
 * and D72's rule that a cancelled meeting is announced rather than deleted). The 2026-11-07 social is the first
 * row this happened to, and it is why the preview that showed a Nov 7 meetup no longer does.
 *
 * 2026-11-15 (round-4 design brief §5, §6, rulings D-B and D340): two rules in this file changed shape, and
 *  every sentence below that predates that date describes what shipped **up to** it.
 *   - The full width used to print three sign-up links inside every row; a row now carries **one action** — the
 *     per-date sign-up, painted by `EventRow.astro` — and the club's standing resources (the chat, the group,
 *     the calendar file) render **once per page**, after the last row, through this file's one
 *     `<SignUpLinks item={…} />`. `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`'s `splitPaths` is the split, so the page and
 *     `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` cannot disagree about which half is which (D-B).
 *   - The compact width used to open with a sentence about where the answer lives; the homepage now states the
 *     answer itself, in `src/components/HeroSection.astro`, from the same `scheduleWindow(upcoming(), todayIn())`
 *     call the nav chip makes (D340, which reverses D306's blanket "`/schedule` is the only surface that prints
 *     a date" for one computed statement on the home page). The sentence this replaces is deleted here rather
 *     than reworded, so the compact band prints no lede while it has rows.
 *   - `data-art-cap="323"` stays on the `<ul>` as a probe handle for `design/schedule-geometry.mjs`; no row
 *     paints a plate any more (D-D), which is what `check:row-geometry` now fails a painted `PS-4` on.
 *
 * The file name stays `Schedule.astro` even though the compact width now carries the join band, and the
 * section's id stays `when-we-meet` even though the heading beside it now reads "Come once": both are probe
 * handles — `design/grill/rhythm-variants.mjs` and `round14-options.mjs` select the landing-page section by
 * that id. Renaming either is a separate change with its own reference sweep (the same ruling that kept
 * `WhyJoinUs.astro` named).
 */
type Props = {
  /** `compact` = the homepage's join band and a pointer at the page of record, printing no date. `full` = `/schedule`. */
  variant?: 'full' | 'compact';
  /** How many rows to render. Unset means the full variant's windowed list (`HORIZON_WEEKS`, `MAX_ROWS`, D238). */
  limit?: number;
};

const { variant = 'full', limit } = Astro.props;
const compact = variant === 'compact';

// The schedule page takes the windowed list; the homepage points at it and prints no date (D306). Both read one
// `upcoming()` call, which reads `data/events.json`, which is what `/events.ics` publishes — so the homepage's
// count of remaining dates and `/schedule`'s rows cannot drift apart.
const all = upcoming();
// `todayIn()` supplies the snapshot's own zone, so the window is measured from the same "today" the rows are
// compared against (D188: a page and its gate reading different clocks is how a date goes missing).
const win = scheduleWindow(all, todayIn());
const items = typeof limit === 'number' ? all.slice(0, limit) : win.shown;
// How many open dates the compact card is NOT showing. Counted off the same call, so the mark that says
// "there is more" cannot outrun the snapshot it came from.
const hidden = Math.max(0, all.length - items.length);
// The window adds a state the empty block has to name honestly: dates exist, and every one of them is past the
// horizon this page can show. Printing "No date is on the calendar yet" over that snapshot states a claim the
// file contradicts (D238).
const beyondWindow = items.length === 0 && win.hiddenHorizon > 0;
// What the page left out, assembled as a sentence where the counts are (D238): a window nothing mentions reads as
// "this club is quiet" rather than "this page is short". Built in the frontmatter rather than in the markup so the
// words come from the same plan that did the hiding, and so the footer stays one paragraph with one way out — the
// calendar feed — instead of two conditional clauses competing with a link for the reader's attention.
const omitted = [];
if (win.hiddenHorizon > 0) {
  omitted.push(`This page lists the next ${HORIZON_WEEKS} weeks, and ${win.hiddenHorizon} more ${win.hiddenHorizon === 1 ? 'date is' : 'dates are'} booked past ${formatDate(win.horizonEnd, 'wide')}.`);
}
if (win.hiddenCap > 0) {
  omitted.push(`It lists at most ${MAX_ROWS} dates, and ${win.hiddenCap} more ${win.hiddenCap === 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} inside that window.`);
}
const omittedText = omitted.join(' ');
// D397: where the zone word goes is a property of the venue grouping, not of row position. The group key is
// the venue's own name — the text before the first comma, which `src/lib/content.ts` documents as "name,
// street, city" — and the same split is what `EventRow.astro` writes into its `data-venue-key` handle, so
// the page and `scripts/check-row-geometry.mjs` agree on the grouping instead of each inventing one.
const venueKey = (it: UpcomingItem) => String(it.venue ?? '').split(',')[0].trim();
const zoneStarts = (() => {
  const out: boolean[] = [];
  let prev: string | null = null;
  for (const it of items) {
    const k = venueKey(it);
    out.push(k !== prev);
    prev = k;
  }
  return out;
})();
// q25 ("How do I get to the venue?"), D375. `node design/visitor-questions.mjs` says the question belongs on
// `/` and `/dates`; D372 answered it on `/join`, the page a visitor reaches after they have already decided
// to come, which the probe prices as ANSWERED-ELSEWHERE - a placement defect, kept apart from a missing fact
// because the two need different work. So the question gets a home on each route it belongs on, **worded per
// surface**: the list on `/dates` prints an address per line and can point at it, the compact card on `/` has
// no address of its own (the hero names the place, a street address is a row's), so its words name the page
// that prints one. Nothing here is invented: "no door of its own" is the same clause D372 grounds in
// `src/content.json`'s `venues.value` and `venues._rule` - the pool a date gets a place from is coffee shops,
// bakeries and breweries - and "no pin", "no route" is the absence D372 verified (`grep` for a map host in
// `dist/client` returns nothing), which is why no `URLs.*` key, no map binding and no absolute URL is added.
// Round-6 review (2026-11-16), `/dates`/Remove: "no page here draws a route to it" is a disclaimer about what
// this site can do, not a fact about getting there (D387). Round-7 review (2026-11-18), same route, same Remove
// column (**D431**): what remains after that cut — "The street address printed with each date is the address to
// give directions to." — describes the page's own layout to the reader, which is the same class of sentence the
// round-6 read cut above it. q25's answer is the address itself, and it is printed in the row, beside the date;
// the row needs no sentence about rows. Nothing about getting there is lost: `src/lib/ics.mjs` still puts the
// venue's street, city and state in `LOCATION`, and the site still prints no map link (D372's absence, re-verified
// by this round's census - `design/review-round-7-census.mjs` rows D5 and D6 return zero anchors for the four map
// hosts a directions link could name).
const empty = content.schedule?.empty;
const venueLine = value(empty?.venueLine);
// The status word distinguishes "nothing scheduled" from "we have not wired the calendar yet" — the first
// is a fact about the club, the second is a fact about this page, and only the first may be stated as the
// club's. With no source configured the block says what the page knows: dates live on Meetup.
let status = scheduleConfigured() ? (empty?.status ?? 'Nothing scheduled') : 'Dates live on Meetup';
let emptyTitle = empty?.title ?? 'No date is on the calendar yet.';
let emptyBody = empty?.body ?? 'Dates are set in the club\'s Discord and posted on Meetup, where the calendar and the sign-up live.';
if (beyondWindow) {
  // Same notice grammar `check:render` measures (status word, lede title, body, dated line) — only the words
  // change, because the fact is different: the calendar has dates, this page just cannot show them yet.
  // `beyondWindow` cannot be true with no open date, so `firstOpen` is read through a local rather than a `!`.
  const far = win.firstOpen;
  status = 'Past this page’s window';
  // Date-free on purpose: this block also renders on the homepage, which prints no date at all (D306).
  emptyTitle = 'The next date is past the window this page shows.';
  emptyBody = `That is more than ${HORIZON_WEEKS} weeks out, and this page lists the ${HORIZON_WEEKS} weeks it can show at once. The calendar file carries every date the organizer has made, however far out.`;
}

// The join band's copy, read from src/content.json with its source, exactly as `JoinCommunity.astro` read
// it: where meetings are held (`venues`), and what the roster says and when it was read (`membership` —
// D136: the count is dated, it has no '+' on it, and the regular-attendee figure is deliberately not
// published). The join prose around it stayed in markup, where it shipped: a new slot in content.json
// without a source would be an `openSlots` hole for `check:content` to report.
// Round-6 review (2026-11-16), Home/Add (D385): one number about the room. It is the site's only painted
// headcount, and it is a sourced slot in `src/content.json` rather than a sentence in markup, which is what
// `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs`'s headcount rule now requires (D265, D385). `venues` and `membership` keep
// their slots and their sources; they simply stop being painted on `/`.
const turnout = value(content.turnout);

// D117: a path needs a page, and it has one now, so the compact band may point at it. The path moved from
// `/schedule` to `/dates` under D373 (the round-5 read's Batch D: the nav, the band and the page's own h1 all
// said `Dates` while the URL said `schedule`); the binding name stayed `scheduleHref` because it is a handle,
// not a path, and renaming a handle is a different change from renaming a route.
const scheduleHref = '/dates';
// When the copy was taken. This is the same stamp every published `DTSTAMP` derives from (`check:ics`'s
// dtstamp rule asserts it), so the sentence under a date and the calendar file a subscriber holds cannot
// disagree about when the club's calendar was last read.
const copied = copiedOn();
// The heading is the page's own h1 on /dates and a section h2 on the homepage, where the hero owns the h1.
// The id stays `when-we-meet` on both surfaces even where the heading beside it now reads "Dates": it is a
// probe handle (design/grill/round14-options.mjs selects the section by this exact attribute), and the rule
// for a probe handle is that it does not follow the copy.
const headingId = 'when-we-meet';
// The heading this component owns is now the **page's own** heading only. Round-8 review (2026-11-18),
// Home copy line: "delete the H2 'When we meet' OR move line 7-9 under it. Do not leave a 'when' heading
// with no when in it." The operator's answer is the first option — delete the heading and the promise under
// it, and keep `/` date-free in the band — so the compact width renders no heading of its own here: a
// heading whose section prints no date is a "when" heading with no when in it (D306 took the date out of
// this band on 2026-11-14, and D340 put the one stated date in the hero). The band's section keeps its
// accessible name, because the card under it now carries `id={headingId}` on its own heading, and every
// probe handle that selects this section by `section[aria-labelledby="when-we-meet"]` still resolves —
// a handle does not follow the copy (D173), and the id stays `when-we-meet` for the same reason.
const Heading = 'h1';
// 2026-11-14 (S040, second design read §1): the eyebrow is gone from both surfaces. `check:naming` (D309) used
// to require the nav's word in the opening block, and the eyebrow was how this section satisfied it - the h1
// keeps its voice ("When We Meet") and the eyebrow carried the name. Two things changed: the read's rule is that
// nothing sits above an h1, and the gate now enforces that, so the eyebrow cannot come back. On `/schedule` the
// lede under the heading already says "These dates are copied from the club's own calendar", which names the
// page in the same block; on the homepage the band's heading IS the nav's word, "Dates", so a visitor who
// clicked "Dates" finds a heading that says "Dates" - and `check:naming`'s new alias rule (a page may not use
// another page's h1 as its own h2) is exactly what this band was violating.
// 2026-11-15 (round-3 read §4, S043): the page of record is named by the nav. `/schedule`'s own h1 used to be
// "When We Meet" while the nav, the <title> and the exit band all said "Dates" - four surfaces, two names, which
// is exactly the alias defect `check:naming` was built for (D309). The name goes on the page that is the page;
// the voice goes to the homepage band, where a section heading is prose rather than a page name. The order also
// removes the collision the read spotted: a section on `/` can no longer wear `/schedule`'s h1 as its own h2.
const headingText = 'Dates';
// The compact lede used to be the join band's sentence, kept verbatim when the bands merged (D228): "One place to
// say you are coming, one place to argue. Neither takes a commitment, and one evening is enough to tell whether
// this is your kind of room." The operator asked for the join copy rewritten (2026-11-07), and the clause that did
// not hold up is the last one: it promises a verdict about the reader's own taste from a single evening, which is
// the page telling someone how they will feel. It also talked about the site's two surfaces ("one place ... one
// place") on a band whose job is to get someone to a table. What replaced it said the two facts this band can back
// up - where the sign-up is, and that the date above it came from the club's own calendar - and then what the thing
// being decided about actually is.
//
// That third sentence is deleted (operator ruling, 2026-11-08, in the review that also named the descriptor), and
// it is deleted here rather than reworded because the ruling is about length, not wording - the band's job is the
// two facts and the buttons. What removing it bought, measured as the round's reversal option `band-restore`
// putting the sentence back: +34px of page at 1240, +68 at 640, +101 at 390, +169 at 320, and the compact band
// going from 3 line boxes to 2 at 1240, 4 to 2 at 640, 6 to 3 at 390 and 8 to 3 at 320
// @measured 2026-11-08 by `npm run round16` (design/grill/round16/report.json). The same four numbers were
// measured from the other direction on 2026-11-07 (−34 / −68 / −101 / −169); re-measuring them against this build
// is what says the record's claim is still true after the wordmark step and the descriptor both changed. Note that
// this sentence existed only on the homepage: the full-page lede below never carried it, so `/schedule` loses
// nothing. The 2026-11-07 pricing of "math" against "mathematics" inside this clause (+28px at 1240) is history
// rather than a live trade - the noun is not on the homepage any more.
// D273 (2026-11-11): the lede no longer names a single platform. "The sign-up is on Meetup" was true of every
// printed date the day it was written, and it is the sentence a visitor carries into the row below, where the
// cluster can say Meetup, Discord, or neither (S019). A lede that names one site when the row under it offers
// two is the same defect class as a button labelled "Details and sign-up" that happens to go to Meetup. The
// claim the band can back up is the one the resolver decides per date: each printed date links to whatever
// sign-up page the club has posted for it.
// D371 (round-5 communication read B2, q18) asked where the file's reach is answered, and `windowSentence` was
// the one call both the page and the file went through. Round-7 review (2026-11-18) closed the page half of that
// answer (**D431**): the sentence it printed — "The club\u2019s dates run from Nov 7, 2026 through Dec 19, 2026."
// — reads as a claim about the club's dates, and the rows under it run Nov 21 → Dec 19, because Nov 7 and Nov 11
// are cancelled and D223 keeps cancellations off the page. A stated range that disagrees with the visible list is
// not a style preference; the review called it "the kind of small wrongness that costs a lot of trust". The
// reach stays **inside the file** (every published `DESCRIPTION` still carries the generated sentence, and
// `check:ics`'s `calendar-window` rule still fails a `VEVENT` that loses it), so q18's answer still lives where
// the subscriber holds it. What the page states instead is what its own rows support: the omission paragraph
// below, which is generated only when the window rule actually hides a row.
const rhythm = rhythmSentence(snapshot.events);
// Round-6 review, `/dates`/Revise (D405): "the format words are never defined on this page. A visitor arriving
// from search learns what a 'Study session' is only by going to `/join`. Add five words at first use." The
// first use on this page **is** the rhythm sentence above - it is what says "for a social", "for a study
// session" - so the definition goes in the lede immediately after it, and not in a row. The words are the two
// `SERIES_VOCABULARY` names `src/lib/event-groups.mjs` owns, and the clauses are the same two definitions
// `src/pages/join.astro` writes under "What we do", shortened; `check:content`'s near-duplicate rule is per
// built file, so one definition on each of two pages is not a defect the gate has to be talked into accepting.
// No cadence word here (`src/lib/cadence.mjs`), and no claim past what the row titles and the feed's own
// DESCRIPTIONs already carry (D92).
// Round-8 review (2026-11-18), `/dates` copy line: the lede's one long paragraph becomes three short ones,
// and the two formats get their own sentences — "A social is an afternoon around a table, nothing to prepare.
// A study session is an evening working side by side." The semicolon that chained them into one 22-word
// sentence is the thing being deleted; the words are `src/lib/event-groups.mjs`'s two series names kept as
// they are, and the two clauses are `join.astro`'s own definitions shortened (D405), so neither page invents
// a format. `check:content`'s near-duplicate rule is per built file, so the definition living on each of two
// pages is not a defect the gate has to be talked into accepting.
const kindDefinitions = 'A social is an afternoon around a table, nothing to prepare. A study session is an evening working side by side.';
const lede = compact
  // 2026-11-15 (D340, round-4 §5): the sentence that used to live here — "The next date, where the club meets,
  // and where to sign up for it are all on the schedule." — is DELETED, not reworded. A page that says where the
  // answer is, instead of what the answer is, has not answered (the brief's own words). The answer now lives
  // above the fold, in `HeroSection.astro`, computed from the same window call the nav chip makes; the band
  // keeps its two quiet paths, because "there is more" and "here is the file" are still this band's job.
  // Null with rows, so the <p> renders nothing rather than an empty paragraph; the empty-state branch keeps its
  // own sentence, which is a claim about a calendar with no dates in it.
  ? (items.length
      ? null
      : 'The club\u2019s dates come from its own calendar.')
  : // Round-6 review, `/dates`/Add: the rhythm is the page's opening line, because this is the page a planner
    // lands on. It is generated from the rows below it (D383), so it is the same sentence the hero prints, on a
    // different page - `check:content`'s near-duplicate rule is per built file, so two pages saying one generated
    // thing is not a defect the gate has to be talked into accepting.
    [rhythm, kindDefinitions, 'These dates are copied from the club\u2019s own calendar.',
      'Each one links to the page the club posts its sign-up on.']
      .filter((s) => s)
      .join(' ');
---

{/* `py-8` (32px), not `py-12`: round 18 (2026-11-17) asked whether the emptiness is answered with less of the
    thing that made it rather than with more colour, and the operator accepted the row that made the three below-fold
    bands and the margins inside them come off one step of the spacing scale (S061). At 1240 that row measured a
    168px shorter page and 24.3% of it in empty runs, against the shipped page's 168px more page and 28.5% empty;
    its cost was the reader meeting 8px above 11 blocks instead of 48. See DESIGN.md §Spacing and
    docs/reply-to-the-composition-review-round-18-2026-11-16.md. The inner `mt-6` keeps its step: the row named the
    bands' air, the card margins and the copy margins, not this divider. */}
<section class="bg-paper py-8" aria-labelledby={headingId}>
  <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="text-center">
      {/* Written out rather than assembled with `class:list={[...]}`: `check:classes` reads the literal word
          `list` out of that syntax and reports it as a class that generates no CSS, so the assembled form
          buys a false failure. A conditional template string keeps every token literal in the source the
          gate reads. */}
      {/* Full width only: `/dates` is the page that prints dates, so it opens with its own name. The compact
          width's heading is deleted (see the note on `Heading` above); the compact card below carries the
          `when-we-meet` id so the section still names itself. */}
      {!compact && (
        <Heading id={headingId} class="font-extrabold text-ink text-display-1">{headingText}</Heading>
      )}
      {/* D340 (2026-11-15): the compact band has no lede while it has rows, so the paragraph is conditional —
          an empty <p> under a heading is a hole the reader pays for and `check:render` would measure. */}
      {lede && <p class={`mt-4 leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body max-w-measure mx-auto ${compact ? 'text-lede' : 'text-body'}`}>{lede}</p>}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6">
      {items.length === 0 && (
        // The chosen empty state (round 7, option A) in the shipped notice grammar: status word, `--lede`
        // title, two-line body, the dated line, then the paths out. One copy of this block for both widths:
        // an empty calendar is empty on either surface, and the two paths out (Meetup, the chat) are the
        // whole answer on both. `min-h-20` is not needed: this block is prerendered at its full height, so
        // there is no arrival to shift the page.
        //
        // One boundary (operator ruling, 2026-11-03): the ring is the notice's edge and the only edge in it,
        // so the paths out sit BELOW the box rather than inside it. `role="status"` is what makes that block
        // measurable — `check:render` holds it to the notice grammar (no fill, 1px ring at the ratified
        // contrast, 48px+) and fails any filled or ringed action that ever moves back inside. Before this
        // change the primary button sat inside the ring: one box, two edges.
        <div class="max-w-measure">
          <div role="status" class="inset-shadow-rule rounded-control px-4 py-6">
            <p class="text-label font-medium text-ink-3">{status}</p>
            <h3 class="mt-1 text-lede font-bold text-ink font-body">{emptyTitle}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{emptyBody}</p>
            {venueLine && <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{venueLine}</p>}
            {/* D306: an empty calendar still renders this block on both widths, so it must not print a date either -
                that was the 2026-11-14 audit's third finding (with no open date, `/` would have painted "built Nov 14,
                2026" and `/schedule` a provenance date with no `data-date` handle). Ages carry the same freshness. */}
            {/* D322 (2026-11-14 design read §D6): one provenance format for the whole site - an age inside 30
                days, the calendar date beyond it. `allowDate: !compact` is not a second format; it is D306's
                rule expressed as an argument, because `check:dated-surfaces` refuses any date-shaped string on
                `/` and lists `/schedule` as the one surface that may carry one. */}
            <p class="mt-2 text-label text-ink-3 tabular-nums">This page was built {provenanceStamp(new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10), { allowDate: !compact })}{snapshot.source?.fetchedAt ? `, and the club’s calendar was last read ${provenanceStamp(String(snapshot.source.fetchedAt).slice(0, 10), { allowDate: !compact })}.` : '; no calendar source is wired to it yet.'}</p>
          </div>
          {/* The primary button belongs to the band, and the compact band has one below with the chat path
              beside it. Printing it in the notice too would put two `data-control="primary"` controls in one
              section — the gate measures each at 48px, but a band with two primaries has no primary. */}
          {!compact && (
            <div class="mt-4">
              <a
                href={`${config.URLs.meetupGroup}/join`}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                data-control="primary"
                class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 rounded-control text-label font-semibold text-band-text bg-ink hover:bg-accent-strong focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent transition"
              >
                Join on Meetup<span class="sr-only">{newTabQualifier('Join on Meetup', PLATFORMS.meetup)}</span>
              </a>
            </div>
          )}
          <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
            Want to ask before you come? <a href="/chat" class="text-ink underline hover:text-accent focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">Ask in the chat</a> —
            someone from the club reads it.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}


      {/* The two quiet paths this band used to print here moved below the card (D388); the empty-state branch
          below keeps its own paths, because there the notice is the content. */}

      {!compact && items.length > 0 && (
        <>
          <ul class="space-y-4" data-art-cap="323">
            {/* D397 (was `first={i === 0}`, D327): the zone word opens each venue group, not each page. */}
            {items.map((item, i) => <EventRow item={item} zone={zoneStarts[i]} />)}
          </ul>
          {/* D218's dated line, once for the list rather than once per row: "is this current?" is asked of the
              calendar, not of a row. The stamp is the feed's own `source.fetchedAt` (`copiedOn()`), which is
              what every published `DTSTAMP` in `/events.ics` derives from \u2014 so the page and the file a
              subscriber holds cannot disagree about when the club was last read. */}
          {copied && (
            <p class="mt-2 tabular-nums"><span class="text-label text-ink-3">Copied from the club’s own calendar {copied}</span></p>
          )}
          {/* What the window hid, as its own paragraph now that the standing row owns the calendar path. */}
          {omittedText && (
            <p class="mt-2 max-w-measure text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{omittedText}</p>
          )}
          {/* Round-4 brief §6, ruling D-B (2026-11-15): the standing row, ONCE per page, after the last row.
              The chat, the group and the calendar file are properties of the club, not of a date, so they used to
              be paid five times on this page (15 outbound links) and are now paid once (5 row primaries + Discord
              + Facebook + the club's own /events.ics). `items[0]` is the first date the page prints, so the
              stamps under the last row describe a date the rows above were built from, and `check:rsvp-page`
              still keys the cluster on a printed date. `/events.ics` lives in this row rather than in a second
              paragraph below it: one path to one file, and the sentence under it is about that path. */}
          <SignUpLinks item={items[0]} />
          {/* Round-7 review (2026-11-18), `/dates`/Remove (D431): the reach sentence and the address meta-note are
              both gone from the page. The reach stays inside the file, where the subscriber holds it (`src/lib/ics.mjs`
              + `check:ics`'s `calendar-window` rule); the address is stated in each row. What keeps the page honest
              about the gap between its rows and the file's is `omittedText` above - generated from the same plan that
              hid rows, and empty when the window hid none. `scripts/check-schedule-window.mjs` no longer demands a
              reach sentence here; it still fails one that is printed and is not the generated one, so a hand-written
              window cannot come back while the gate is green. */}
        </>
      )}

      {/* The join band's mechanics, absorbed into this section on 2026-11-07 (D228). The tinted card is a
          FILL, not a boundary, so the section still has one keylined box (the preview card) and the page has
          one dark region (the footer). PS-3 stays named here for the same reason PS-4 stays named in the
          rows: `check:space` ranks the reserved places by `data-slot`, and `npm run round13`/`round14`
          refuse to run once the page stops naming its own photo places.
          The two paths out stand BELOW the card, on the page's own ground, and that is a ruling rather than
          a taste: the secondary is `bg-paper inset-shadow-rule`, so its ring has its own `paper` fill (3.66)
          on one side and whatever it stands on on the other; inside this card it would stand on `panel`
          (3.85) @measured 2026-11-14 by node design/grill/palette-remap.mjs — two grounds, which D149-3c
          forbids whatever the numbers say. The 2.88 design/records/0018 measured was the pre-D316 `rule`; the
          straddle is the half of that record that still applies. */}
      {compact && (
        <>
          <div class="mt-2 bg-panel rounded-card p-6 sm:p-8">
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-center">
              <div>
                {/* Round-8 review (2026-11-18), Home: with the band's "When we meet" deleted, this is the
                    heading the compact section opens under, so it takes the section's probe id and the level
                    the ladder needs (an h3 with no h2 above it would skip a level, which `check:outline`
                    fails). Its classes are unchanged: this is a level and an id, not a restyle. */}
                <h2 id={headingId} class="text-subhead font-semibold text-ink">How it works</h2>
                {/* Round-6 review (2026-11-16), Home/Revise + "what to do this week" item 4 (D388): this heading
                    spent itself on the website's data policy - 15 venues, the roster's age, no map pin - which is
                    the material the reviewer called "written for an auditor, not for a visitor". What ships here is
                    the prediction the peers make and this site never did: what happens in the first ten minutes,
                    what to bring, what is expected, and one number about the room. The four sentences deleted here
                    are not reworded; `venues`, `venueCount` and `membership` keep their slots in
                    `src/content.json` with their sources, they simply stop being painted on `/`.
                    The coffee line and the turnout figure are operator-granted draft claims (D381, D385): the
                    ledger in docs/reply-to-the-communication-review-round-6-2026-11-16.md names both and the
                    command that corrects them. The one number is data, not markup, so the operator's corrected
                    figure is a one-line edit to `src/content.json`. */}
                <div class="mt-2 space-y-3 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
                  <p>Walk in, find a table, buy a coffee if you want one — that is the rent.</p>
                  <p>Bring nothing, prepare nothing, and bring a question if you have one.</p>
                  {turnout && <p>{turnout}</p>}
                </div>
              </div>
              {/* PS-3, the join media column (round 10 ruling: reserve it now). Its box measured **532x56 at
                  1240** (one paragraph) inside a grid row **326** tall, and **310x85 at 390**, **240x113 at
                  320**, where the grid is one column. @measured 2026-11-03 by check:space — the paragraph that
                  used to be inside this column moved into the copy beside it, so this box needs re-measuring
                  before any round quotes it again.
                  The join path stays whether or not the plate does: art may add to the join path here, never
                  replace it (D149-1b, D94). */}
              <div data-slot="PS-3">
                <PhotoSlot
                  slot="join"
                  ratio="532 / 128"
                  caption="the club mid-session, wide crop (532x128)"
                />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          {/* Round-6 review, Home/Revise ("a heading that promises a fact and delivers navigation") and week item
              4 (D388): the two quiet paths come after the card that says what a meeting is, so the section opens
              with content rather than with navigation. D139's one-primary rule is untouched: these are inline
              links, not controls. The empty-state branch keeps its links where they were, because there the notice
              IS the content. */}
          {items.length > 0 && (
            <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
              {hidden > 0 && (
                <a href={scheduleHref} class="text-label font-medium text-ink underline decoration-rule underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">
                  {/* The label has to match what the target page actually lists: `/dates` shows a window, not the
                      whole future, so promising "all fourteen dates" on a page that prints eight would be a link that
                      lies about its own destination (D238). */}
                  {/* Round-7 communication review (2026-11-18), Home/Revise ("four names for one route — use 'dates'
                      everywhere, matching the nav chip"): the count leaves the label, the word stays. The count was
                      never wrong — it is computed off the same `win` the target page prints, which is D238's rule —
                      but it is a number a visitor reads while deciding to leave, and D410 already declined that kind
                      of count on this page for that reason. Where the number is what makes the label honest (the
                      window hides rows, so the page lists the *next* N rather than everything) it stays. */}
                  {win.hiddenHorizon + win.hiddenCap > 0 ? `Next ${win.shown.length} dates` : `All dates`} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                </a>
              )}
              {/* MS36 (D444): the calendar path's own hue, as on `SignUpLinks.astro`'s anchor — `text-path-calendar`
                  replaces `text-ink` here so the two "Add to a calendar" anchors on this page carry the one
                  reserved hue for the one route it was reserved for (`/events.ics`, global.css:102). On `paper`, on
                  both anchors: this row stands below the tinted card, on the page's own ground, which is also why
                  D149-3c's straddle never applies to it. Hover stays the stated `hover:text-accent`. */}
              <a href="/events.ics" class="text-label font-medium text-path-calendar underline decoration-rule underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">
                Add to a calendar <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
            </div>
          )}

          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
            {/* Was the page's second primary, labelled "Join on Meetup" and pointing at the group: two
                primaries on one surface and both of them Meetup is the defect D303 names. It is the same
                action as the hero's now — the club's own route, which explains the three kinds of joining —
                so it is a secondary here and the homepage keeps exactly one primary. The platform-specific
                pair that used to live here is one click further in, on /join. */}
            <a
              href="/join"
              data-control="secondary"
              class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 text-label font-semibold rounded-control text-ink bg-paper inset-shadow-rule hover:bg-tint focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent transition"
            >
              Join the club
              <svg class="ml-2 -mr-1 w-4 h-4" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true">
                <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M10.293 3.293a1 1 0 011.414 0l6 6a1 1 0 010 1.414l-6 6a1 1 0 01-1.414-1.414L14.586 11H3a1 1 0 110-2h11.586l-4.293-4.293a1 1 0 010-1.414z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
              </svg>
            </a>
            <a
              href={config.URLs.discordInvite}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              data-control="secondary"
              class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 text-label font-semibold rounded-control text-ink bg-paper inset-shadow-rule hover:bg-tint focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent transition"
            >
              Join the chat<span class="sr-only">{newTabQualifier('Join the chat', PLATFORMS.discord)}</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </>
      )}

      {/* 2026-11-15 (round-4 §6, D-B): this paragraph is gone. It carried the second `/events.ics` path on the
          page and the sentence about Google, Apple and Outlook, and both moved into the standing row — one path
          to one file, one sentence about it, rendered once after the last row. What stayed here is the window's
          own confession about what it hid, now its own paragraph above that row (D238, D150). */}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
